{"key": "Medical::Medical-f79d978d", "time": "2026-10-09T15:22:17+00:00", "raw": {"candidates": [{"content": {"role": "model", "parts": [{"text": "When a lung nodule is found on imaging, additional scans may be performed to assess whether it is cancer, or the patient may be referred for lung cancer screening if it is unlikely to be cancer. If the nodule seems to be cancer, a care team of experts decides the course of care by assessing the patient's lung cancer risk, reviewing images and requesting further imaging if cancer is suspected, and performing a biopsy if needed.", "thoughtSignature": ""}]}, "finishReason": "STOP", "groundingMetadata": {"groundingChunks": [{"retrievedContext": {"uri": "Medical.txt", "title": "Medical.txt"}}], "groundingSupports": [{"segment": {"endIndex": 193, "text": "When a lung nodule is found on imaging, additional scans may be performed to assess whether it is cancer, or the patient may be referred for lung cancer screening if it is unlikely to be cancer"}, "groundingChunkIndices": [0]}, {"segment": {"startIndex": 195, "endIndex": 429, "text": "If the nodule seems to be cancer, a care team of experts decides the course of care by assessing the patient's lung cancer risk, reviewing images and requesting further imaging if cancer is suspected, and performing a biopsy if needed"}, "groundingChunkIndices": [0]}]}}], "usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 90, "candidatesTokenCount": 256, "totalTokenCount": 1217, "trafficType": "ON_DEMAND", "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 90}], "candidatesTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 256}], "thoughtsTokenCount": 871}, "modelVersion": "gemini-3.8-flash", "createTime": "2026-10-09T15:22:00.839632Z", "responseId": "mAbJatCfM86w3dAPqoOouQc"}}
{"key": "Medical::Medical-d5964ccf", "time": "2026-10-09T15:22:29+00:00", "raw": {"candidates": [{"content": {"role": "model", "parts": [{"text": "Community Health Advisory: Lung Cancer Prevention and Early Detection\n\nLung cancer forms in the cells that line the airways of the lungs, and anyone can develop the disease. The leading cause and biggest risk factor is smoking tobacco, with the risk rising the longer and more frequently an individual smokes. Non-smokers are also placed at risk through secondhand smoke exposure. Other critical risk factors include being over 70 years of age, past radiation therapy to the chest, family genetics, and exposure to environmental carcinogens such as radon, asbestos, arsenic, beryllium, cadmium, chromium, nickel, coal smoke, soot, silica, diesel fumes, and particulate air pollution. \n\nFor prevention, individuals who do not smoke should avoid starting, while current smokers are strongly urged to quit, as quitting reduces the likelihood of new lung tumors developing. Healthcare providers can assist with cessation counseling and medications, and patients can ask about clinical trials studying chemopreventive agents.\n\nThe goal of lung cancer screening is early detection before any symptoms appear, which is when treatment is most successful and can help prevent death. Screening is not recommended for everyone, but rather is intended for people assessed as being at high risk based on their individual risk factors and family medical history. Community members should consult their healthcare providers to determine whether they qualify for screening. \n\nRegarding symptom awareness, lung cancer is frequently diagnosed only after symptoms appear or when routine blood tests turn up abnormal findings. Symptoms vary depending on where a tumor grows or spreads, and tumors can prevent the lungs from getting necessary air. Additionally, some individuals may develop symptoms stemming from paraneoplastic syndromes, which are abnormal bodily responses triggered by the cancer. Anyone experiencing concerning changes or who may be at elevated risk should promptly consult their medical care team.", "thoughtSignature": ""}]}, "finishReason": "STOP", "groundingMetadata": {"groundingChunks": [{"retrievedContext": {"uri": "Medical.txt", "title": "Medical.txt"}}, {"retrievedContext": {"uri": "Medical.txt", "title": "Medical.txt"}}, {"retrievedContext": {"uri": "Medical.txt", "title": "Medical.txt"}}, {"retrievedContext": {"uri": "Medical.txt", "title": "Medical.txt"}}], "groundingSupports": [{"segment": {"startIndex": 71, "endIndex": 172, "text": "Lung cancer forms in the cells that line the airways of the lungs, and anyone can develop the disease"}, "groundingChunkIndices": [0, 1]}, {"segment": {"startIndex": 174, "endIndex": 308, "text": "The leading cause and biggest risk factor is smoking tobacco, with the risk rising the longer and more frequently an individual smokes"}, "groundingChunkIndices": [0]}, {"segment": {"startIndex": 310, "endIndex": 379, "text": "Non-smokers are also placed at risk through secondhand smoke exposure"}, "groundingChunkIndices": [0]}, {"segment": {"startIndex": 381, "endIndex": 682, "text": "Other critical risk factors include being over 70 years of age, past radiation therapy to the chest, family genetics, and exposure to environmental carcinogens such as radon, asbestos, arsenic, beryllium, cadmium, chromium, nickel, coal smoke, soot, silica, diesel fumes, and particulate air pollution"}, "groundingChunkIndices": [0, 1, 2]}, {"segment": {"startIndex": 686, "endIndex": 868, "text": "For prevention, individuals who do not smoke should avoid starting, while current smokers are strongly urged to quit, as quitting reduces the likelihood of new lung tumors developing"}, "groundingChunkIndices": [2]}, {"segment": {"startIndex": 870, "endIndex": 1019, "text": "Healthcare providers can assist with cessation counseling and medications, and patients can ask about clinical trials studying ch